 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "**Mechanism:** Werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects that read a `contextvars.ContextVar`. The proxy looks up the current value each time you touch it and forwards the access to it.\n\n**`src/tessel/globals.py`**\n- `_app_var` (line 40) is a `ContextVar[AppContext]` named `\"tessel.app_ctx\"`. It is the single source of truth.\n- `active_app` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`, so it resolves to `AppContext.app`, the `Tessel` instance.\n- `g` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`, so it resolves to `AppContext.g`, an `_AppCtxGlobals`.\n- `request` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`, so it resolves to `AppContext.request`, a `Request` from `src/tessel/wrappers.py`.\n- `app_ctx` and `session` use the same pattern. Each proxy has an `unbound_message`, which is the error you get when you use it outside a context.\n\n**`src/tessel/ctx.py`**\n- `AppContext` holds `app`, `g`, `request` and `session`.\n- Pushing a context calls `_app_var.set(self)` (line 433) and stores the returned token in `_cv_token`.\n- Popping calls `_app_var.reset(self._cv_token)` (line 498), which restores the previous context.\n\nBecause it is a `ContextVar`, each thread or async task sees its own current context. Request and app contexts have been merged into one `AppContext`. `request_ctx` is only a deprecated alias for `app_ctx`.",
